{"authored" [{"question" "q-7110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t)=6t+6{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=2$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6t+6{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $x(0)=2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3(0)^{2}+2(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=3{t}^{2}+2{t}^{3}+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-7110" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)={e}^{3t},$ where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=0$ and velocity $v=1$ when $t=0,$ the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$x(t)=$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 1 & =\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int {e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{2}{3}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{0}+\\frac{2}{3}(0)+K \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{9}+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{1}{9}{e}^{3t}+\\frac{2}{3}t-\\frac{1}{9}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7a0b1ed9ffb8f9da9229cc9c42f583e6acc8f07ec669b0a20abf12286cd89bcb" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-351730" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/state.json" "file_sha256" "6fa5b1f294951f26b7115ab34eec78d48686c673a1ec39849b48c6b3a2c095fa" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with velocity $v(t) = 2t+3t^2,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle is at the position $x=1$ when $t=0,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-566.json" "file_sha256" "7d957ebb615aae355106efc3d71f66ff16facecf5bb29f73b038af9a289c8c8c" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+{t}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $x(0) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(0)^{2}+(0)^{3}+C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is\n\n$$\nx(t) = t^3+t^2+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-566.json" "file_sha256" "7d957ebb615aae355106efc3d71f66ff16facecf5bb29f73b038af9a289c8c8c" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t) = 1-2t,$ where $t\\geq 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=2$ when $t=1,$ calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t >0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-567.json" "file_sha256" "fa565d9f6fbb776da62c27108739e91c883fcc178cb4da6538af09758ab0ed49" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (1-2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =t-{t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(1) = 2.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =(1)-(1)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t) = t-t^2 + 2.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (t-{t}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}+2t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(1) = 1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{2}(1)^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+2(1)+K \\\\ K & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t) = \\dfrac{1}{2}t^2-\\dfrac{1}{3}t^3 +2t -\\dfrac{7}{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/example-567.json" "file_sha256" "fa565d9f6fbb776da62c27108739e91c883fcc178cb4da6538af09758ab0ed49" "basis" 1157 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012107/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=1$ and velocity $v=-\\frac{1}{2}$ when $t=1$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{4}{(t+1)^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{2}{(1+1)^{2}}+C \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}.$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t+1}+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(1)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{2}{1+1}+K \\\\ 1 & =1+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7915f30689183a7758777ce35ef9acf0ce1509658cd8c4b3a8bb25fffecc58b0" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{1}{t+1}+\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{2}{t+1}-1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x(t)=\\frac{4}{(t+1)^{2}}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$, where $t\\ge 0$ is the time. If the particle has position $x=\\frac{1}{16}$ and velocity $v=\\frac{1}{4}$ when $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, calculate the position $x$ of the particle at time $t>0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{4}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{1}{4}+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).$ \n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K,$ we use the fact that $x(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\frac{1}{16}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{16} & =0+C \\\\ K & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/state.json" "file_sha256" "37d51446b8b92ec0cad35ec3abeb6288a39eca6691a2b98cd6a918996197fd05" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-102994" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037927/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7915f30689183a7758777ce35ef9acf0ce1509658cd8c4b3a8bb25fffecc58b0" "basis" 1282 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/